which  makes  visible  all  packets  logged by the packet filter,
Logged packets can easily be monitored in real time  by  invoking
on the interface, or stored to disk using The pflog0 interface is
created  automatically  at  boot if both and are enabled; further
instances can be created using Each packet retrieved on this  in‐
terface  has  a  header  associated with it of length This header
documents the address family, interface name, rule  number,  rea‐
son,  action,  and direction of the packet that was logged.  This
structure,   defined   in   looks   like   struct   pfloghdr    {
        u_int8_t        length;               sa_family_t     af;
        u_int8_t        action;           u_int8_t        reason;
        char            ifname[IFNAMSIZ];
        char            ruleset[PF_RULESET_NAME_SIZE];
        u_int32_t       rulenr;            u_int32_t       subru‐
lenr;          uid_t           uid;          pid_t           pid;
        uid_t           rule_uid;
        pid_t           rule_pid;            u_int8_t        dir;
        u_int8_t        pad[3]; }; Create a interface and monitor
all packets logged on it: # ifconfig pflog1 up #  tcpdump  ‐n  ‐e
